  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Anomalously warm low-level thicknesses and\ndewpoints beneath strong ridging aloft will continue to keep \ntemperatures well above normal with potential record breaking \nheat continuing today, with heat index values peaking at \n105-110 deg this afternoon for most of the area. Heat Advisory\ncontinues for most of the area, with highs in the 90s and\ndewpoints in the 70s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A strong upper level trough will develop off \nthe U.S. East Coast over the weekend, and this will help drive a\nstrong cold front south through the Carolinas later today and\ntonight. This plus increased forcing along the cold front and \nother lingering boundaries should support an increased risk of \ndeep convection, and less limiting factors, compared to previous\ndays. PW's/TD's inc further and with lowering hts aloft, \ninstability will be very strong. In fact, vals will be well \nabove the avg max for first week of September according to the \nKMHX sounding climatology, with ML CAPE vals advertised by the \nRRFS/ECM/NAM3k reaching or exceeding 4 kJ/kg. If convective \ntiming aligns with fropa/shortwave passage in the afternoon, a \nsignificant severe weather signal may be met as this aligns with\nstout deep layer shear, possibly in excess of 35-45kt. Machine \nlearning severe probs for damaging wind continue to tick up for \nparts of ENC. In addition to the severe threat, will have to \nwatch for potential flash flood threat, as PWs surge to 2.25\" or\nhigher, and with the expected numerous convection, very heavy \nrain rates are possible with deeper convection and lend to \ninstances of flash flooding, especially with potential for \ntraining of storms. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Behind the front, temperatures and humidity \nwill finally get knocked down closer to normal for early \nSeptember, with more comfortable conditions lasting into early \nnext week. Still some residual moisture lingering on Sunday and\nmay see some sct showers, but overall trend is for drier esp as\nwe begin the work week with pops less than 20% lasting through\nmid week. Highs in the mid 80s with lows in the 60s is fcst and\nis where our temps should be for the first half of September.",
